# naming
## A name
Prisca is in the counting room. She has a tablet with a blank face and a lamp turned down, and Hanno beside her with the stylus ready. They have set a table, which none of them will eat from. She does not ask how the day went. She asks only one question.
"What is the firm's name?"
$ do discover("collis")
You have been carrying it all day. You have known it, in a way, since the road to Ostia. You do not remember deciding.
"Collis," you say.
"A hill," says Prisca.
"A hill," you say. "A small one."
She looks at you for a moment longer than she needs to. Then she picks up the tablet, and sets it down again without writing.
?if b1.prisca_terms == "defied"
  "A hill with a village on it," says Prisca. "Which is a very short walk for an enemy, when the enemy has an hour and a sharp mind."
?else
  "A hill with a village on it," says Prisca, gently. "Which is a short walk for an enemy who is patient."
?end
"I will not ask you to change it," she says. "I will ask you to decide what you want it to be."
:the_name
* Keep the name, and let the link stand
  $ b1.collis_name = "tribute"
  $ humanity += 1
  $ secrecy -= 1
  $ bond.prisca += 1
  "Then keep it," says Prisca. "It is yours. If it is traced, it is traced. I would rather have a name that means something than a name that is safe." She writes it in a firm square hand, and Hanno blots it, and it is done: Collis Trading, of the Argiletum, dealers in oil and wine and other things. It sounds, you think, like a family that has been in business for a hundred years.
  You think of the hill, and the barn, and the old press. You think: let them come.
* Hide the link: a different name on the door, and the village stays a secret
  $ b1.collis_name = "hidden"
  $ do hide_collis(true)
  $ standing -= 2
  $ cunning_brutal -= 2
  $ secrecy += 3
  "Then we shall be a firm with no story," says Prisca, "and a firm with no story is a firm with a secret. It will cost us a little trust in the Forum. I have found that trust is cheaper than blood." She nods to Hanno, and he writes a different name, a bland one, with the village folded out of it like a fold in a letter.
  It is the safest thing. It is also the first lie you have told with a ledger.
---
$ do rest()
Hanno reads you the first day's figures in his dry slow voice. They are not large. They are, he says, the right shape.
?if rested("income") > 0
  There is a small purse on the table the next evening, the first coin of the business to come in. You put your hand on it and do not know what to say.
?end
Then he lays down the stylus, and for the first time since you have known him, he smiles. It is a very small smile. It is a smile that, you realize, has been waiting a very long time for something to be pleased about.
There is a letter on the table. It has been delivered by hand, in the afternoon, by a man in a plain grey cloak who did not wait for an answer. The wax has no seal. The hand is small and quick and perfectly clear.
?if b1.galerius == "hostile"
  > I understand that a new firm has opened in the Argiletum, and that it is managed with remarkable energy by day. I had not known that the Merchant house had mortal agents of such vigor. I shall make a note of it. I make a note of everything. G.
?elif b1.galerius == "wary"
  > I hear that the Merchant house has found a mortal man of business. I am happy for it. I will, of course, wish to meet him. I shall call, at an hour convenient to him. G.
?else
  > A note of congratulation, from the Senate's census: I hear the Merchant house has opened a trading firm. Such enterprise in the made is rare, and I shall follow it with interest. It is, of course, only an interest. G.
?end
Prisca reads it over your shoulder. She says nothing for a time. Then she says, quite lightly, "He has noticed that the man of business is not a man who has ever been seen at the house." She picks up the abacus. "We shall have to be very careful."
She turns it over in her hands.
"But that, I think, is a very good problem to have."
